+## Mechanical eligibility
+
+Mechanical eligibility is a hard safety gate and is separate from social willingness. A challenge is refused before duel state is created when the player or Sim is unavailable/dead, the target is a remote human, required `Character`/`NPC`/`SimPlayer` bindings are missing, the Sim is too far away, camp mode is active, another duel is active, or verified real combat is already targeting the player/candidate.
+
+The current code uses only signals already present in the 0.3 implementation: `SimPlayer`, `Character.Alive`, `NPC.ThisSim`, `NPC.CurrentAggroTarget`, `GameData.AttackingPlayer`, scene-local active objects, and the existing COOP compatibility check. There is no guessed Erenshor “busy”, “travel”, or “zoning” field.
+
+## Social willingness
+
+A mechanically eligible party Sim keeps the established 0.3 behavior and accepts.
+
+A mechanically eligible nearby non-party Sim uses a deterministic game-side willingness policy. It may consider only verified state:
+
+- current Sim HP readiness;
+- a two-minute per-Sim recent accepted-duel cooldown;
+- player/Sim level difference when a runtime `Level` member can actually be read;
+- the Sim’s `Rival` flag when present.
+
+The result is an `accept`, `decline`, `decline_low_health`, `decline_recent_duel`, or `decline_level_mismatch` decision. The numeric score is never shown to the player. `Rival` is a bounded positive modifier and never overrides hard eligibility, the recent-duel cooldown, or the hard low-health reluctance.
+
+Resource readiness, non-combat travel/activity, Deep Sims familiarity, and verified prior Deep Sims duel history are intentionally not used yet because Practice Duels does not currently have a stable public signal/API for them. `decline_busy` is reserved for a future verified non-combat activity signal.
+
+A declined challenge does not snapshot health/effects, change targets/aggro, disable procs, or alter follow/guard state.
 
